1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,312
Chapter 38: Contraception.

3
00:00:05,312 --> 00:00:21,887
Intrauterine Devices Progestin-Only Contraceptives Combination Hormonal Contraceptives Barrier Methods Fertility Awareness-Based Methods Spermicides Emergency Contraception References The puerperium offers an excellent opportunity to provide effective contraception.

4
00:00:21,887 --> 00:00:28,850
For mothers who are nursing exclusively, ovulation during the first 10 weeks after delivery is unlikely.

5
00:00:28,850 --> 00:00:36,525
Nursing, however, is not a reliable method of family planning for women whose infants breastfeed only during the day.

6
00:00:36,525 --> 00:00:44,625
Moreover, waiting for first menses involves a risk of pregnancy, because ovulation usually antedates menstruation.

7
00:00:44,625 --> 00:00:51,325
Certainly, after the first menses, contraception is essential unless the woman desires pregnancy.

8
00:00:51,325 --> 00:00:56,325
Nearly half of all pregnancies each year in the United States are unintended .

9
00:00:56,325 --> 00:01:01,962
These may follow contraceptive method failure or stem from lack of contraceptive use.

10
00:01:01,962 --> 00:01:06,450
For those seeking contraception, effective options are available .

11
00:01:06,450 --> 00:01:12,987
Among these, estimated failure rates of perfect and typical use during the first year differ widely .

12
00:01:12,987 --> 00:01:21,487
Efficacy tiers reflect these failure rates, and implants and intrauterine devices (IUDs) are found in the top tier .

13
00:01:21,487 --> 00:01:28,725
They effectively drop unintended pregnancy rates and are considered long-acting reversible contraception (LARC).

14
00:01:28,725 --> 00:01:34,700
Clinicians provide counseling on all options and encourage LARC for appropriate candidates .

15
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:37,175
Table 38-1.

16
00:01:37,175 --> 00:01:49,687
Contraceptive Failure Rates of Reversible Methods During the First Year and Use Ratesa No contraceptive method is completely without side effects, but contraception usually poses less risk than pregnancy.

17
00:01:49,687 --> 00:01:55,675
However, some disorders or medications can raise the risks of certain contraceptives.

18
00:01:55,675 --> 00:02:06,175
The World Health Organization (2015) has provided guidelines for the use of effective reversible contraceptive methods by women with various health conditions.

19
00:02:06,175 --> 00:02:10,625
Individual countries have subsequently modified these guidelines.

20
00:02:10,625 --> 00:02:21,875
The United States Medical Eligibility Criteria (US MEC) was updated in 2016 by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention and is available at their website .

21
00:02:21,875 --> 00:02:45,087
In the US MEC, reversible contraceptive methods are organized into six groups: levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system (LNG-IUS), copper intrauterine devices (Cu-IUDs), implants, depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA), progestin-only pills (POPs), and combination hormonal contraceptives (CHCs).

22
00:02:45,087 --> 00:02:51,887
This last group includes combination oral contraceptives (COCs), rings, and patches.

23
00:02:51,887 --> 00:02:56,862
For a given health condition, each method is categorized 1 through 4.

24
00:02:56,862 --> 00:03:13,137
The score describes the safety profile for a typical woman with that condition: (1) no restriction of method use, (2) method advantages outweigh risks, (3) method risks outweigh advantages, and (4) method poses an unacceptable health risk.

25
00:03:13,137 --> 00:03:24,012
Alternatively, depending on the underlying disorder or patient desire, male or female sterilization may be a preferred or recommended permanent contraceptive method.

26
00:03:24,012 --> 00:03:27,575
These options are discussed in Chapter 39.

27
00:03:27,575 --> 00:03:29,962
Intrauterine Devices.

28
00:03:29,962 --> 00:03:38,562
The five IUDs currently approved for use in the United States are chemically active and continually elute either copper or a progestin.

29
00:03:38,562 --> 00:03:45,437
All have a flexible, T-shaped, polyethylene frame compounded with barium to render them radiopaque.

30
00:03:45,437 --> 00:03:57,000
The progestin-eluting devices are similarly shaped, but each differs by size, string color, longevity, and presence or absence of a silver band at the junction of the stem and arms .

31
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,437
Among these, smaller-sized devices are thought to better fit a nulliparous uterus .

32
00:04:02,437 --> 00:04:13,337
In contrast, the copper device, the T380A IUD named ParaGard, contains a thin copper strand wound around its stem and a copper bracelet on each arm.

33
00:04:13,337 --> 00:04:15,800
Table 38-2.

34
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:20,525
Properties of Intrauterine Devices # Contraceptive Action.

35
00:04:20,525 --> 00:04:28,087
The contraceptive mechanism of IUDs is not precisely defined, but prevention of fertilization is now favored.

36
00:04:28,087 --> 00:04:36,112
Within the uterus, an intense local endometrial inflammatory response is induced, especially by the Cu-IUD.

37
00:04:36,112 --> 00:04:42,837
Inflammatory fluid fills the uterine cavity and fallopian tubes to decrease sperm and egg viability .

38
00:04:42,837 --> 00:04:45,287
Also, in the unlikely

39
00:04:45,436 --> 00:04:52,298
event that fertilization does occur, the same inflammatory actions are directed against the blastocyst.

40
00:04:52,248 --> 00:05:07,161
With the LNG-IUS, progestin release atrophies the endometrium to hinder normal implantation and creates scant viscous cervical mucus to obstruct sperm motility (Apter, 2014; Silverberg, 1986).

41
00:05:07,161 --> 00:05:16,261
The above effects are considered primary because ovulation inhibition is inconsistent with the LNG-IUS and lacking with the Cu-IUD .

42
00:05:16,261 --> 00:05:19,611
# Method-specific Adverse Effects.

43
00:05:19,611 --> 00:05:28,186
Ectopic Pregnancy In the past, IUDs were perceived to increase the risk of ectopic pregnancy, but this has since been clarified.

44
00:05:28,186 --> 00:05:38,411
Specifically, IUDs provide effective contraception and lower the absolute number of ectopic pregnancies by half compared with the rate in noncontracepting women .

45
00:05:38,411 --> 00:05:45,161
But, the IUD mechanisms of action are more effective in preventing intrauterine implantation.

46
00:05:45,161 --> 00:05:55,073
Thus, if an IUD fails, a higher proportion of pregnancies are likely to be ectopic (Furlong, 2002; Teal, 2019).

47
00:05:55,073 --> 00:06:00,848
Lost Device Expulsion of an IUD from the uterus is most common during the first month.

48
00:06:00,848 --> 00:06:11,348
Thus, women are examined approximately 4 to 6 weeks following IUD insertion, usually after menses, to identify the tails trailing from the cervix.

49
00:06:11,348 --> 00:06:16,923
Following this, a woman is instructed to palpate the strings each month after menses.

50
00:06:16,923 --> 00:06:29,548
Regardless of IUD type, the cumulative 3-year expulsion rate approximates 10 percent following nonpuerperal insertion (Madden, 2014; Simonatto, 2016).

51
00:06:29,548 --> 00:06:38,323
If the tail of an IUD cannot be visualized, the device may have been expelled, may have perforated the uterus, or may be malpositioned.

52
00:06:38,323 --> 00:06:46,223
Alternatively, the device may be normally positioned with its tail folded within the endocervical canal or uterine cavity.

53
00:06:46,223 --> 00:06:56,911
To investigate, after excluding pregnancy, a cytological brush can be twirled within the endocervical canal to entangle the strings and bring them gently into the vagina.

54
00:06:56,911 --> 00:07:06,236
If unsuccessful, the uterine cavity is probed gently with a Randall stone clamp or with a specialized rod with a terminal hook to retrieve the strings.

55
00:07:06,236 --> 00:07:10,648
One should not assume that a device has been expelled unless it was seen.

56
00:07:10,648 --> 00:07:23,086
Thus, if tails are not visible and the device is not felt by gentle probing of the uterine cavity, transvaginal sonography (TVS) can be used to ascertain if the device lies within the uterus.

57
00:07:23,086 --> 00:07:31,786
Although traditional TVS will document IUD position adequately in most cases, three-dimensional TVS offers improved views .

58
00:07:31,786 --> 00:07:39,136
If sonography is inconclusive or if no device is seen, a plain radiograph of the abdominopelvis is taken.

59
00:07:39,136 --> 00:07:49,148
Computed tomography (CT) scanning or magnetic resonance (MR) imaging is an alternative depending on coexisting pregnancy and study access.

60
00:07:49,148 --> 00:07:54,623
MR imaging at 1.5 or 3 Tesla with an IUD in place is safe .

61
00:07:54,623 --> 00:08:06,723
Perforation During sounding or IUD insertion, the uterus may be perforated, which is identified by the tool traveling farther than expected based on initial bimanual uterine examination.

62
00:08:06,723 --> 00:08:18,236
Rates approximate 1 case per 1000 insertions, and risks include puerperal insertion and breastfeeding (Barnett, 2017; Kaislasuo, 2012).

63
00:08:18,236 --> 00:08:27,286
With acute perforation, the fundus is the more common site, and bleeding is often minimal due to myometrial contraction around the puncture site.

64
00:08:27,286 --> 00:08:34,936
If no brisk bleeding is noted from the os following removal of the wounding tool, patient observation alone is reasonable.

65
00:08:34,936 --> 00:08:44,398
Rarely, lateral perforations may lacerate the uterine artery, and subsequent heavy bleeding prompts laparoscopy or laparotomy for control.

66
00:08:44,398 --> 00:08:50,123
With occult perforation, a device can penetrate the myometrium to varying degrees.

67
00:08:50,123 --> 00:08:58,436
Abdominal pain, uterine bleeding, or missing strings are clues, and imaging described in the last section is a primary step .

68
00:08:58,436 --> 00:09:04,848
A device with an arm partially embedded can sometimes be removed transcervically with steady traction.

69
00:09:04,848 --> 00:09:11,548
Otherwise, IUDs with a mainly intrauterine location are usually removed hysteroscopically.

70
00:09:11,548 --> 00:09:18,473
Devices that have nearly or completely perforated through the uterine wall are more easily extracted laparoscopically.

71
00:09:18,473 --> 00:09:26,911
Devices often embed on posterior cul-de-sac structures and omentum, but bowel and bladder perforations are possible (Şengül,

72
00:09:27,076 --> 00:09:31,076
2014; Zeino, 2011).

73
00:09:31,026 --> 00:09:36,913
Menstrual Changes Dysmenorrhea and irregular bleeding can complicate IUD use.

74
00:09:36,913 --> 00:09:47,063
These can be treated with some degree of success by nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) or tranexamic acid, which is an antifibrinolytic .

75
00:09:47,063 --> 00:09:57,076
Of IUD types, heavy bleeding more often complicates Cu-IUD use and may cause iron-deficiency anemia, for which oral iron salts are given.

76
00:09:57,076 --> 00:10:11,138
With the LNG-IUS, irregular spotting for up to 6 months after placement often gives way to progressive amenorrhea, which is reported by approximately 10 percent of women after year 1 and 35 percent after 3 years .

77
00:10:11,138 --> 00:10:15,263
This is frequently associated with improved dysmenorrhea.

78
00:10:15,263 --> 00:10:27,638
Infection The risk of upper genital tract device-related infection is greatest during the first 3 weeks following IUD insertion (Farley, 1992; Turok, 2016).

79
00:10:27,638 --> 00:10:33,638
Pathogens include Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Chlamydia trachomatis, and vaginal flora.

80
00:10:33,638 --> 00:10:42,051
Women at risk for sexually transmitted diseases (STDs) should be screened either before or at the time of IUD insertion .

81
00:10:42,051 --> 00:10:49,788
That said, device insertion need not be delayed while awaiting STD or Pap test results in asymptomatic women .

82
00:10:49,788 --> 00:10:56,413
If these bacteria are subsequently found, the IUD may remain and treatment may be prescribed.

83
00:10:56,413 --> 00:11:01,713
Routine antimicrobial prophylaxis before insertion is not recommended .

84
00:11:01,713 --> 00:11:06,576
Bacterial endocarditis prophylaxis is not needed with insertion .

85
00:11:06,576 --> 00:11:21,076
After the first month, infection risk is not elevated in IUD users who would otherwise be at low risk of STDs. Correspondingly, IUDs cause little, if any, increase in infertility rates in these low-risk patients .

86
00:11:21,076 --> 00:11:33,776
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) recommends that women at low risk for STDs, including adolescents, be considered good candidates for IUDs.

87
00:11:33,776 --> 00:11:43,101
The IUD is also safe and effective in women with immunosuppression, including human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection .

88
00:11:43,101 --> 00:11:48,576
Moreover, IUD use does not appear to raise HIV acquisition rates .

89
00:11:48,576 --> 00:11:55,776
If infection does develop, it may take several forms and typically requires broad-spectrum antibiotics.

90
00:11:55,776 --> 00:12:02,013
Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) without abscess is treated with antibiotics.

91
00:12:02,013 --> 00:12:08,751
There are theoretical concerns that a coexistent IUD may worsen the infection or delay resolution.

92
00:12:08,751 --> 00:12:23,013
A provider may choose to remove an IUD in this setting, although growing evidence supports allowing device retention during treatment in those with mild or moderate PID (Curtis, 2016a; Tepper, 2013).

93
00:12:23,013 --> 00:12:29,738
If infection fails to improve during 48 to 72 hours of treatment, the device is removed.

94
00:12:29,738 --> 00:12:38,613
Tuboovarian abscess can complicate PID and is treated aggressively with intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics and IUD removal.

95
00:12:38,613 --> 00:12:44,538
Last, septic abortion mandates immediate uterine evacuation and antibiotics.

96
00:12:44,538 --> 00:12:51,851
Actinomyces israelii is a gram-positive, slow-growing, anaerobic, indigenous vaginal bacterium.

97
00:12:51,851 --> 00:13:01,676
It is frequently identified in the vaginal flora or on the Pap smears of IUD users (Curtis, 1981; Kim, 2014).

98
00:13:01,676 --> 00:13:12,376
If found, an asymptomatic woman may retain her IUD and does not require antibiotics (Lippes, 1999; Westhoff, 2007a).

99
00:13:12,376 --> 00:13:21,851
However, with infection in a woman who harbors Actinomyces species, the device is removed and antibiotics with gram-positive coverage are given.

100
00:13:21,851 --> 00:13:29,826
Early findings with infection include fever, weight loss, abdominal pain, and abnormal uterine bleeding or discharge.

101
00:13:29,826 --> 00:13:38,963
Pregnancy with an IUD For women who become pregnant despite an IUD, ectopic pregnancy and pelvic infection each must be excluded.

102
00:13:38,963 --> 00:13:47,038
Pregnant women with a retained IUD and infection are treated with broad-spectrum antibiotics and prompt uterine evacuation.

103
00:13:47,038 --> 00:13:56,276
For those with intrauterine pregnancy without infection, the IUD tail can be grasped, and the IUD removed by gentle outward traction.

104
00:13:56,276 --> 00:14:02,676
This action reduces rates of subsequent abortion, chorioamnionitis, and preterm birth .

105
00:14:02,676 --> 00:14:12,151
Specifically, in one cohort, a 54-percent abortion rate and 17-percent preterm delivery rate was noted if the device remained in situ.

106
00:14:12,151 --> 00:14:20,113
More favorably, rates of 25 percent and 4 percent, respectively, resulted from prompt Cu-IUD removal .

107
00:14:20,113 --> 00:14:26,301
Few data guide management with the LNG-IUS, and most practice extrapolates from

108
00:14:26,452 --> 00:14:28,439
copper devices.

109
00:14:28,389 --> 00:14:34,314
If the tail is not visible, attempts to locate and remove the device may result in abortion.

110
00:14:34,314 --> 00:14:47,527
Some case reports and small series describe sonography or hysteroscopy to assist difficult device removals, but this is not our practice (Pérez-Medina, 2014; Schiesser, 2004).

111
00:14:47,527 --> 00:14:55,727
In women who give birth with a device in place, appropriate steps should be taken at delivery to identify and remove the IUD.

112
00:14:55,727 --> 00:14:58,989
# Intrauterine Device Insertion.

113
00:14:58,989 --> 00:15:03,677
Timing Before insertion, IUD contraindications are sought .

114
00:15:03,677 --> 00:15:07,477
Candidates are counseled, and written consent obtained.

115
00:15:07,477 --> 00:15:09,977
Table 38-3.

116
00:15:09,977 --> 00:15:20,464
Contraindications to IUD Use To reduce expulsion rates, IUD insertion traditionally has followed complete uterine involution and is termed interval placement.

117
00:15:20,464 --> 00:15:33,577
Instead, immediately following miscarriage, surgical abortion, or delivery, an IUD may be inserted in the absence of overt infection (Roe, 2019; Whitaker, 2018).

118
00:15:33,577 --> 00:15:44,164
Also, early insertion 1 week after mifepristone and completed medical abortion has been described (Sääv, 2012; Shimoni, 2011).

119
00:15:44,164 --> 00:15:53,889
Compared with interval insertion, the IUD expulsion rate is slightly higher with immediate placement, defined as the first 10 minutes after placenta delivery.

120
00:15:53,889 --> 00:16:00,814
The highest rates are with early placement, defined as later than 10 minutes but within the first month postpartum .

121
00:16:00,814 --> 00:16:07,677
The higher US MEC scores seen in Table 38-4 reflect these early-placement expulsion risks.

122
00:16:07,677 --> 00:16:23,502
However, the number of women in immediate-placement groups who ultimately receive and retain an IUD is greater than in groups scheduled for interval placement, because some fail to return for insertion (Bednarek, 2011; Chen, 2010).

123
00:16:23,502 --> 00:16:26,077
Table 38-4.

124
00:16:26,077 --> 00:16:39,477
U.S. Medical Eligibility Criteria Category of Contraceptive Methods Related to Breastfeeding and Time After Delivery Postpregnancy Insertion Techniques With immediate insertion, techniques depend on uterine size.

125
00:16:39,477 --> 00:16:46,902
After firsttrimester evacuation, the IUD can be placed using the manufacturer’s standard instructions.

126
00:16:46,902 --> 00:16:53,939
If the uterine cavity is larger, the IUD can be placed using ring forceps with sonographic guidance .

127
00:16:53,939 --> 00:17:03,227
Immediately following vaginal or cesarean delivery, an IUD can be placed by a hand, by its inserter tube, or by ring forceps .

128
00:17:03,227 --> 00:17:08,789
The arms of the IUD need not be folded into the inserter tube prior to insertion.

129
00:17:08,789 --> 00:17:17,839
During cesarean delivery placement, the hand or inserter travels through the unsutured hysterotomy opening to deposit the device at the fundus .

130
00:17:17,839 --> 00:17:24,602
A second hand cupping the outer fundus can provide back pressure and stabilize the uterus during insertion.

131
00:17:24,602 --> 00:17:28,727
Strings are gently directed but not pulled toward the cervix.

132
00:17:28,727 --> 00:17:31,252
Figure 38-1.

133
00:17:31,252 --> 00:17:36,564
A. Intrauterine device (IUD) insertion at cesarean delivery.

134
00:17:36,564 --> 00:17:40,689
The device inserter guides the device to the uterine fundus.

135
00:17:40,689 --> 00:17:46,302
A hand at the fundus can provide back pressure to stabilize the uterus during insertion.

136
00:17:46,302 --> 00:17:49,989
B. IUD insertion after vaginal delivery.

137
00:17:49,989 --> 00:17:53,927
Ring forceps direct the device to the uterine fundus.

138
00:17:53,927 --> 00:17:59,102
Back pressure against the fundus by an abdominal hand can help guide positioning.

139
00:17:59,102 --> 00:18:05,277
Reproduced with permission from Stuart GS, Hoffman BL: Puerperal sterilization.

140
00:18:05,277 --> 00:18:14,589
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

141
00:18:14,589 --> 00:18:18,514
New York, McGraw Hill; 2017.

142
00:18:18,514 --> 00:18:29,202
For instrumented insertion following vaginal delivery, the clinician resterilizes the vulva and changes gloves after placental delivery but before perineal repairs.

143
00:18:29,202 --> 00:18:33,789
The anterior lip of the floppy cervix is held with ring forceps.

144
00:18:33,789 --> 00:18:41,977
A second ring forceps gently grasps the IUD stem, guides it up into the uterine cavity, and deposits it at the fundus.

145
00:18:41,977 --> 00:18:47,614
During forceps removal, the jaws remain open to avoid ensnaring the strings.

146
00:18:47,614 --> 00:18:55,514
For manual insertion following vaginal delivery, the provider holds the IUD between fingers to deposit the device.

147
00:18:55,514 --> 00:19:01,802
In either case, back pressure against the fundus by an abdominal hand can guide fundal positioning .

148
00:19:01,802 --> 00:19:16,039
Interval Insertion Technique For placement not related to pregnancy, insertion near the end of normal menstruation, when the cervix is usually softer and somewhat more dilated, may be easier and also helps exclude early pregnancy.

149
00:19:16,039 --> 00:19:18,127
However, for the

150
00:19:18,292 --> 00:19:24,729
woman who is sure she is not pregnant and does not want to be pregnant, insertion is done at any time.

151
00:19:24,679 --> 00:19:36,342
To ease insertional pain, applying lidocaine-prilocaine cream locally or placing paracervical blockade can be helpful (Akers, 2017; Samy, 2019).

152
00:19:36,342 --> 00:19:41,379
Bimanual pelvic examination delineates uterine position and size.

153
00:19:41,379 --> 00:19:46,354
Abnormalities are evaluated, as they may contraindicate insertion.

154
00:19:46,354 --> 00:19:54,054
Mucopurulent cervicitis or significant vaginitis is appropriately treated and resolved before IUD insertion.

155
00:19:54,054 --> 00:20:00,092
The ectocervix is cleansed with an antiseptic solution, and sterile instruments are used.

156
00:20:00,092 --> 00:20:08,854
A tenaculum is placed on the cervical lip, and the endocervical canal and uterine cavity are straightened by applying gentle outward traction.

157
00:20:08,854 --> 00:20:14,167
A uterine sound is guided into the cavity to identify its direction and depth.

158
00:20:14,167 --> 00:20:21,779
Specific steps of Cu-IUD and LNG-IUS insertions are outlined in their respective package inserts.

159
00:20:21,779 --> 00:20:26,954
Following insertion, only the threads should be visible trailing from the cervix.

160
00:20:26,954 --> 00:20:33,554
These are trimmed to allow 3 to 4 cm to protrude into the vagina, and their length is recorded.

161
00:20:33,554 --> 00:20:37,604
An oral NSAID can be used for cramping after insertion .

162
00:20:37,604 --> 00:20:44,717
If improper device positioning is suspected, placement should be confirmed, using sonography if necessary.

163
00:20:44,717 --> 00:20:51,554
If the IUD is not positioned completely within the uterus, it is removed and replaced with a new device.

164
00:20:51,554 --> 00:20:56,029
An expelled or partially expelled device should not be reinserted.

165
00:20:56,029 --> 00:20:58,842
Progestin-Only Contraceptives.

166
00:20:58,842 --> 00:21:01,842
# Actions and Side Effects.

167
00:21:01,842 --> 00:21:07,329
Progestin-only contraceptives include implants, injectables, and pills.

168
00:21:07,329 --> 00:21:19,792
These suppress luteinizing hormone (LH) to block ovulation, cervical mucus thickens to retard sperm passage, and atrophy renders the endometrium unfavorable for implantation.

169
00:21:19,792 --> 00:21:26,017
Fertility is restored rapidly following cessation except for DMPA, described later .

170
00:21:26,017 --> 00:21:35,954
After placenta delivery, progesterone withdrawal may contribute to lactogenesis, and early progestin use theoretically could hinder breastmilk establishment.

171
00:21:35,954 --> 00:21:49,054
Although studies support the safety of early puerperal use of progestins, this theoretical risk is still reflected in their higher US MEC score (Carmo, 2017; Phillips, 2016).

172
00:21:49,054 --> 00:21:56,917
For all progestin-only methods, irregular uterine bleeding is the most frequent adverse event prompting discontinuation.

173
00:21:56,917 --> 00:22:00,579
Often, counseling and reassurance is sufficient.

174
00:22:00,579 --> 00:22:09,504
In others, troublesome bleeding may be improved by combining the progestin method with a 2-week course of estrogen or with a short course of NSAIDs .

175
00:22:09,504 --> 00:22:15,117
A few cycles of COCs combined with DMPA or an implant is another option.

176
00:22:15,117 --> 00:22:22,679
Fortunately, with prolonged use, progestins induce endometrial atrophy, which can lead to sustained amenorrhea.

177
00:22:22,679 --> 00:22:26,617
For the informed patient, this is often an advantage.

178
00:22:26,617 --> 00:22:30,379
Iron-deficiency anemia is also less likely.

179
00:22:30,379 --> 00:22:42,242
Most progestin-only contraceptive methods do not significantly affect lipid metabolism, glucose levels, hemostatic factors, liver function, thyroid function, or blood pressure .

180
00:22:42,242 --> 00:22:57,129
However, the increased lowdensity lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol and decreased high-density lipoprotein (HDL) cholesterol levels seen with DMPA are less desirable for women with cardiovascular disease risks .

181
00:22:57,129 --> 00:23:12,542
Risks for genital tract, liver, or breast neoplasia are not increased with progestin-only methods (Samson, 2016; Wilailak, 2012; World Health Organization, 1991a,b, 1992).

182
00:23:12,542 --> 00:23:23,754
Weight gain and bone fracture are not prominent side effects of this contraceptive group, except for DMPA, discussed later (Lopez, 2012, 2013).

183
00:23:23,754 --> 00:23:32,354
Functional ovarian cysts are seen more often in women using progestin-only agents, although intervention is not usually required .

184
00:23:32,354 --> 00:23:41,542
Last, concern for a higher depression rate is not supported by recent studies (Skovlund, 2016; Worly, 2018).

185
00:23:41,542 --> 00:23:44,879
# Progestin Contraindications.

186
00:23:44,879 --> 00:23:54,629
Current pregnancy, breast cancer, acute liver disease, liver tumors, and undiagnosed abnormal genital bleeding are absolute contraindications.

187
00:23:54,629 --> 00:24:06,504
For Nexplanon and DMPA, their manufacturers also add current or past history of thromboembolic disorders (Merck, 2019; Pfizer, 2017).

188
00:24:06,504 --> 00:24:13,742
However, for women with these disorders, the US MEC considers progestin-containing methods category 2 .

189
00:24:13,900 --> 00:24:16,712
# Progestin Implants.

190
00:24:16,662 --> 00:24:25,625
These thin, pliable, progestin-containing cylinders can be implanted in the upper arm to release contraceptive hormone over several years.

191
00:24:25,625 --> 00:24:34,575
Implants are an effective, top-tier method (Mommers, 2012; Sivin, 1998; Steiner, 2010).

192
00:24:34,575 --> 00:24:40,850
These systems are placed subdermally on the inner arm approximately 8 cm from the elbow.

193
00:24:40,850 --> 00:24:46,900
Implants vary in their insertion technique, and manufacturer instructions should be consulted.

194
00:24:46,900 --> 00:24:54,125
After their expiration date, implants are removed, and may be replaced at the same site or in the opposite arm.

195
00:24:54,125 --> 00:25:02,500
Nexplanon is a single-rod etonogestrel implant that releases at least 30 µg of hormone daily and may be used for 3 years.

196
00:25:02,500 --> 00:25:07,900
The inserter design assists with subdermal positioning and averting deeper placement.

197
00:25:07,900 --> 00:25:17,187
Nexplanon replaced Implanon, which is still approved by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) but no longer distributed by the manufacturer.

198
00:25:17,187 --> 00:25:29,375
Levonorgestrel implants are two-rod systems available outside the United States and contain a total LNG dose of 150 mg. Jadelle provides contraception for 5 years.

199
00:25:29,375 --> 00:25:33,900
It is approved by the FDA but not marketed in the United States.

200
00:25:33,900 --> 00:25:38,000
Sino-implant II provides 3 years of contraception .

201
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:46,562
Method-specific Adverse Effects In addition to the hormonal side effects listed earlier, the implants themselves can cause adverse events.

202
00:25:46,562 --> 00:25:56,725
These complicate approximately 1 percent of implant insertions and 5 percent of removals (Creinin, 2017; Reed, 2019).

203
00:25:56,725 --> 00:26:08,537
Of serious events, branches of the medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve and median nerve can be injured during an implant insertion that is too deep or during exploration for implant removal .

204
00:26:08,537 --> 00:26:17,762
Thus, implants found deep in muscle or near neurovascular structures are best removed by surgeons with a clear understanding of upper arm anatomy .

205
00:26:17,762 --> 00:26:24,737
Nonpalpable devices are not uncommon and require imaging for localization prior to attempted removal.

206
00:26:24,737 --> 00:26:29,950
Nexplanon is radiopaque and can be imaged by two-dimensional x-ray views.

207
00:26:29,950 --> 00:26:42,825
Implanon is not radiopaque, and sonography using a 10- to 15-MHz linear array transducer or MR imaging is needed (Correia, 2012; Shulman, 2006).

208
00:26:42,825 --> 00:26:47,425
Rarely, an implant can migrate to distant sites such as the lung .

209
00:26:47,425 --> 00:26:55,362
If imaging fails to locate an implant, etonogestrel blood levels can help verify that the implant is indeed in situ.

210
00:26:55,362 --> 00:27:02,912
This assay must be coordinated with the manufacturer (877-8884231).

211
00:27:02,912 --> 00:27:09,637
Implant Insertion The etonogestrel implant is ideally inserted within 5 days of menses onset.

212
00:27:09,637 --> 00:27:18,725
With LNG-releasing implants, contraception is established within 24 hours if inserted within the first 7 days of the menstrual cycle.

213
00:27:18,725 --> 00:27:30,650
For transitioning methods, an implant is placed on the day of the first placebo COC pill; on the day that the next DMPA injection would be due; or within 24 hours of the last POP .

214
00:27:30,650 --> 00:27:40,112
In women certain that they are not pregnant, insertion at other times of the cycle is followed by an additional method that serves as a back-up method for 7 days.

215
00:27:40,112 --> 00:27:49,837
Postabortion or postpartum, an implant may be inserted before discharge home (Madden, 2012; Sothornwit, 2017).

216
00:27:49,837 --> 00:27:58,062
For insertion, the supine patient positions her nondominant arm against the bed so that the arm is abducted and the elbow is flexed.

217
00:27:58,062 --> 00:28:10,337
With a sterile pen, the insertion site is marked 8 to 10 cm proximal to the humerus’ medial condyle and 3 to 5 cm posterior to the groove between biceps and triceps muscles .

218
00:28:10,337 --> 00:28:18,575
A second mark is placed 4 cm proximal to the first and serves as a guide for the insertion path along the arm’s long axis.

219
00:28:18,575 --> 00:28:22,362
The Nexplanon is inserted using sterile technique.

220
00:28:22,362 --> 00:28:31,200
The area is cleansed aseptically, and a 1-percent lidocaine anesthetic track is injected beneath the skin along the planned insertion path.

221
00:28:31,200 --> 00:28:35,487
The implant is then placed as shown in Figure 38-2.

222
00:28:35,487 --> 00:28:42,912
After placement, both patient and provider should palpate and identify both ends of the 4-cm implant.

223
00:28:42,912 --> 00:28:49,687
To minimize bruising at the site, a pressure bandage is created around the arm and is removed the following day.

224
00:28:49,687 --> 00:28:52,162
Figure 38-2.

225
00:28:52,162 --> 00:28:54,525
Nexplanon insertion.

226
00:28:54,525 --> 00:29:01,875
A sterile pen marks the insertion site, which is 8 to 10 cm proximal to the medial humeral condyle.

227
00:29:01,875 --> 00:29:05,125
A second mark is placed 4 cm

228
00:29:05,284 --> 00:29:08,609
proximally along the arm’s long axis.

229
00:29:08,559 --> 00:29:16,584
The area is cleaned aseptically, and a 1-percent lidocaine anesthetic track is injected along the planned insertion path.

230
00:29:16,584 --> 00:29:24,084
A. The insertion device is grasped at its gripper bubbles found on either side, and the needle cap is removed outward.

231
00:29:24,084 --> 00:29:27,259
The device can be seen within the needle bore.

232
00:29:27,259 --> 00:29:31,396
The needle bevel then pierces the skin at a 30-degree angle.

233
00:29:31,396 --> 00:29:38,359
B. Once the complete bevel is subcutaneous, the needle is quickly angled downward to lie horizontally.

234
00:29:38,359 --> 00:29:46,346
C. Importantly, the skin is tented upward by the needle as the needle is slowly advanced horizontally and subdermally.

235
00:29:46,346 --> 00:29:53,684
D. Once the needle is completely inserted, the lever on the top of the device is pulled backward toward the operator.

236
00:29:53,684 --> 00:29:57,821
This retracts the needle and thereby deposits the implant.

237
00:29:57,821 --> 00:30:01,034
The device is then lifted away from the skin.

238
00:30:01,034 --> 00:30:06,959
After placement, both patient and operator should palpate the 4-cm implant.

239
00:30:06,959 --> 00:30:12,271
With implant extraction, the removal site is first cleansed with antiseptic.

240
00:30:12,271 --> 00:30:18,896
The proximal end of the implant is depressed with a finger to allow the distal end to lift up toward the skin.

241
00:30:18,896 --> 00:30:27,284
After anesthetizing the skin over this bulge, the skin is incised 2 mm toward the elbow along the arm’s long axis.

242
00:30:27,284 --> 00:30:31,496
The implant’s proximal butt is then pushed toward this incision.

243
00:30:31,496 --> 00:30:36,946
Once visible, the implant’s distal end is grasped with a hemostat and removed.

244
00:30:36,946 --> 00:30:43,321
Superficial adhesions surrounding an implant are common and are dissected away with hemostat tips.

245
00:30:43,321 --> 00:30:47,059
# Injectable Progestin Contraceptives.

246
00:30:47,059 --> 00:31:00,646
DMPA, 150 mg every 3 months, and norethisterone enanthate, 200 mg every 2 months, are two intramuscular injectable progestin-only contraceptives used worldwide.

247
00:31:00,646 --> 00:31:06,559
Of the two, DMPA is available in the United States and marketed as Depo-Provera.

248
00:31:06,559 --> 00:31:14,521
DMPA is injected into the deltoid or gluteus muscle, but massage is avoided to ensure that the drug is released slowly.

249
00:31:14,521 --> 00:31:25,021
A subcutaneous version, depo-subQ provera 104, is injected into the subcutaneous tissue of the anterior thigh or abdomen every 3 months.

250
00:31:25,021 --> 00:31:27,884
This form may be self-administered .

251
00:31:27,884 --> 00:31:33,046
Initial injection is given within the first 5 days following menses onset.

252
00:31:33,046 --> 00:31:38,171
Serum levels sufficient for contraception are present by 24 hours.

253
00:31:38,171 --> 00:31:44,146
Thus, no additional contraceptive method is required for initiation within this window.

254
00:31:44,146 --> 00:31:50,396
Limited data support a quick start, or initiation of DMPA regardless of cycle day.

255
00:31:50,396 --> 00:32:06,821
If so implemented, investigators recommend an initial negative pregnancy test result before injection, a supplemental contraceptive method during the 7 days following injection, and a second pregnancy test after 3 to 6 weeks to identify an early pregnancy .

256
00:32:06,821 --> 00:32:11,821
Conceptions during DMPA use do not have higher fetal anomaly rates .

257
00:32:11,821 --> 00:32:21,496
Method-specific Adverse Effects Unique to DMPA, prolonged anovulation can follow discontinuation and results in delayed fertility resumption .

258
00:32:21,496 --> 00:32:28,159
After injections are stopped, a fourth of patients do not resume regular menses for up to 1 year .

259
00:32:28,159 --> 00:32:35,646
Thus, DMPA may be less ideal for women who plan to use birth control only briefly before attempting conception.

260
00:32:35,646 --> 00:32:43,559
As with other progestins, DMPA is not associated with cardiovascular events or stroke in otherwise healthy women.

261
00:32:43,559 --> 00:32:50,384
However, in those with severe hypertension, a higher risk of stroke has been found in DMPA users .

262
00:32:50,384 --> 00:33:02,146
The US MEC authors express concerns regarding DMPA’s hypoestrogenic effects and reduced HDL levels for women with vascular disease or multiple cardiovascular disease risks .

263
00:33:02,146 --> 00:33:08,959
Weight gain is generally attributed to DMPA, and ranges from 1 to 3 kg in the first year .

264
00:33:08,959 --> 00:33:17,796
In long-term users, bone density loss is also greater than in nonusers (Petitti, 2000; Scholes, 1999).

265
00:33:17,796 --> 00:33:26,671
It is somewhat reassuring that bone loss appears to slowly reverse after cessation (Clark, 2006; Scholes, 2002).

266
00:33:26,671 --> 00:33:43,034
In 2004, the FDA added a black box warning to DMPA labeling, which notes that this concern is probably most relevant for adolescents, who are building bone mass, and perimenopausal women, who will soon have increased bone loss during menopause.

267
00:33:43,034 --> 00:33:47,921
Despite this, DMPA should not be restricted in those high-risk groups .

268
00:33:47,921 --> 00:33:50,821
# Progestin-only Pills.

269
00:33:50,821 --> 00:33:53,121
Also called mini-pills,

270
00:33:53,284 --> 00:33:59,521
this group contains norethindrone-only, desogestrelonly, and drospirenone-only pills.

271
00:33:59,471 --> 00:34:07,809
Of these, each norethindrone-only pill provides 0.35 mg of hormone and is taken daily and continuously.

272
00:34:07,809 --> 00:34:12,009
This specific drug does not reliably inhibit ovulation.

273
00:34:12,009 --> 00:34:17,059
Efficacy stems from cervical mucus thickening and endometrial atrophy.

274
00:34:17,059 --> 00:34:23,196
Because mucus changes are not sustained, these pills are best taken at the same time each day.

275
00:34:23,196 --> 00:34:31,646
If the pill is taken even 3 hours late (missed-pill window), a back-up form of contraception is used for the next 48 hours.

276
00:34:31,646 --> 00:34:42,684
In contrast, the drospirenone-only pill marketed as Slynd delivers 4 mg of hormone and is taken for 24 consecutive days out of a 28-day cycle.

277
00:34:42,684 --> 00:34:48,121
Progestin withdrawal during the last 4 days aims to minimize irregular bleeding .

278
00:34:48,121 --> 00:34:55,421
Both drospirenone and desogestrel pills alter mucus and endometrium plus reliably inhibit ovulation.

279
00:34:55,421 --> 00:35:01,871
Slynd offers a 24hour missed-pill window, which mirrors that of COC pills .

280
00:35:01,871 --> 00:35:05,884
Drospirenone is structurally similar to spironolactone.

281
00:35:05,884 --> 00:35:20,159
It displays antiandrogenic activity, provides an antialdosterone action to minimize water retention, and has antimineralocorticoid properties that may, in theory, cause potassium retention and hyperkalemia .

282
00:35:20,159 --> 00:35:30,684
Thus, contraindications are renal or adrenal insufficiency in addition to traditional progestin contraindications, listed earlier (p. 669).

283
00:35:30,684 --> 00:35:39,584
Serum potassium level monitoring is recommended in the first month for patients chronically treated with any drug associated with potassium retention.

284
00:35:39,584 --> 00:35:52,571
These include NSAIDs, angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors, angiotensin II antagonists, heparin, aldosterone antagonists, and potassium-sparing diuretics.

285
00:35:52,571 --> 00:36:01,209
Last, desogestrel-only pills contain 0.075 mg of hormone and are used daily and continuously.

286
00:36:01,209 --> 00:36:04,284
Its missed-pill window is 12 hours.

287
00:36:04,284 --> 00:36:07,496
These are not available in the United States.

288
00:36:07,496 --> 00:36:10,634
Combination Hormonal Contraceptives.

289
00:36:10,634 --> 00:36:13,334
# Mechanism of Action.

290
00:36:13,334 --> 00:36:24,496
The most important contraceptive effect of both the estrogen and progestin components of CHCs is suppression of hypothalamic gonadotropin-releasing factors to inhibit ovulation.

291
00:36:24,496 --> 00:36:32,796
The progestin also thickens cervical mucus to retard sperm passage and renders the endometrium unfavorable for implantation.

292
00:36:32,796 --> 00:36:40,021
Estrogen stabilizes the endometrium, which prevents intermenstrual bleeding—also known as breakthrough bleeding.

293
00:36:40,021 --> 00:36:43,146
This benefit is termed cycle control.

294
00:36:43,146 --> 00:36:48,459
The net effect is an extremely effective yet highly reversible contraceptive method.

295
00:36:48,459 --> 00:36:52,284
# Combination Oral Contraceptive Pills.

296
00:36:52,284 --> 00:36:58,196
Composition COCs are marketed in various estrogen and progestin combinations .

297
00:36:58,196 --> 00:37:04,521
Ethinyl estradiol is the most common estrogen present in formulations in the United States.

298
00:37:04,521 --> 00:37:10,184
Less frequently, mestranol, estetrol, or estradiol valerate is used.

299
00:37:10,184 --> 00:37:18,096
Unwanted effects most often attributed to the estrogen component are breast tenderness, weight gain, nausea, and headache.

300
00:37:18,096 --> 00:37:20,709
Table 38-5.

301
00:37:20,709 --> 00:37:34,046
Estrogen and Progestins Used in FDA-Approved Combination Oral Contraceptive Pills COCs contain one of several progestins that are structurally related to progesterone, testosterone, or spironolactone.

302
00:37:34,046 --> 00:37:42,446
Thus, these progestins bind variably to progesterone, androgen, glucocorticoid, and mineralocorticoid receptors.

303
00:37:42,446 --> 00:37:49,446
These affinities explain many pill-related side effects and are often used to compare one progestin with another.

304
00:37:49,446 --> 00:37:58,534
However, the advantage of one progestin over another is less apparent clinically (Lawrie, 2011; Moreau, 2007).

305
00:37:58,534 --> 00:38:05,309
To minimize adverse effects, estrogen and progestin content in COCs has dropped remarkably.

306
00:38:05,309 --> 00:38:12,659
Currently, the lowest acceptable dose is limited by the ability to prevent pregnancy yet maintain cycle control.

307
00:38:12,659 --> 00:38:22,971
Thus, the daily estrogen content varies from 10 to 50 μg of ethinyl estradiol, and most contain ≤35 μg.

308
00:38:22,971 --> 00:38:28,921
In a few COCs, inert placebo pills have been replaced by tablets containing iron.

309
00:38:28,921 --> 00:38:32,221
These have the suffix Fe added to their name.

310
00:38:32,221 --> 00:38:40,346
Instead, Beyaz and Sayfral have a form of folate—levomefolate calcium—within both its active and placebo pills.

311
00:38:40,346 --> 00:38:45,709
With COCs termed monophasic, the progestin dose remains constant.

312
00:38:45,709 --> 00:38:51,621
In others, the dose frequently is varied, and term biphasic, triphasic, or

313
00:38:51,772 --> 00:38:56,859
quadriphasic is used depending on the number of dose changes within one cycle.

314
00:38:56,809 --> 00:39:01,209
In some formulations, the estrogen dose also varies.

315
00:39:01,209 --> 00:39:11,984
In general, phasic pills aim to reduce the total progestin content and associated side effects per cycle without sacrificing contraceptive efficacy or cycle control.

316
00:39:11,984 --> 00:39:17,534
The theoretical advantage of this approach, however, has not been borne out clinically .

317
00:39:17,534 --> 00:39:26,634
Cycle control also appears similar among mono-, bi-, and triphasic pills (van Vliet, 2011a,b,c).

318
00:39:26,634 --> 00:39:39,584
Administration Hormones are taken daily for a specified time (21 to 81 days) and then replaced by placebo for a specified time (4 to 7 days), which is called the pill-free interval.

319
00:39:39,584 --> 00:39:45,034
During these pill-free days, bleeding prompted by hormonal withdrawal is expected.

320
00:39:45,034 --> 00:39:51,209
With the trend toward lower estrogen doses, follicular growth and ovulation may occur.

321
00:39:51,209 --> 00:39:57,659
To counter this, the active-pill duration in some formulations is extended to 24 days.

322
00:39:57,659 --> 00:40:08,009
In comparison, these 24/4 regimens perform similarly to 21/7 regimens (Anttila, 2011; Marr, 2012).

323
00:40:08,009 --> 00:40:14,447
Longer durations of active hormone are designed to minimize the number of withdrawal bleeding episodes.

324
00:40:14,447 --> 00:40:20,584
This practice has similar efficacy and safety profiles compared with traditional administration .

325
00:40:20,584 --> 00:40:29,709
These extended-cycle products produce a 13-week cycle, that is, 12 weeks of hormone use, followed by a week for withdrawal menses.

326
00:40:29,709 --> 00:40:37,309
Instead, the product Amethyst provides continuous active hormone pills for 365 days each year.

327
00:40:37,309 --> 00:40:44,747
These extended-cycle and continuous regimens may be especially suited for women with significant menstrual symptoms .

328
00:40:44,747 --> 00:40:49,684
Women ideally begin COCs on the first day of a menstrual cycle.

329
00:40:49,684 --> 00:40:53,609
In such cases, a back-up method is unnecessary.

330
00:40:53,609 --> 00:41:02,797
With the more traditional “Sunday start,” women begin pills on the first Sunday that follows menses onset, and a back-up method is added for 1 week.

331
00:41:02,797 --> 00:41:08,859
If menses begin on a Sunday, pills are begun that day and no back-up method is required.

332
00:41:08,859 --> 00:41:15,684
Alternatively, with a quick start, COCs are started on any day, commonly the day prescribed.

333
00:41:15,684 --> 00:41:18,772
A back-up method is added for 1 week .

334
00:41:18,772 --> 00:41:28,247
If the woman is unknowingly already pregnant, COCs are not teratogenic (Rothman, 1978; Savolainen, 1981).

335
00:41:28,247 --> 00:41:33,309
A missed menses after COC initiation should prompt pregnancy testing.

336
00:41:33,309 --> 00:41:38,934
Similar initiation methods are used for contraceptive vaginal rings or patches.

337
00:41:38,934 --> 00:41:44,009
For maximum efficiency, pills are best taken at the same time each day.

338
00:41:44,009 --> 00:41:52,834
If one dose is missed, the missed pill is taken immediately; the scheduled dose for that day is taken on time; and then daily pills are continued.

339
00:41:52,834 --> 00:42:05,084
If two or more doses are missed, the most recent missed pill is taken immediately; the scheduled dose for that day is taken on time; and a back-up method is used for 7 days while daily pills are then continued .

340
00:42:05,084 --> 00:42:12,647
If withdrawal bleeding fails to occur during the pill-free interval, a woman should continue her pills but exclude pregnancy.

341
00:42:12,647 --> 00:42:16,922
With COC initiation, spotting or bleeding is common.

342
00:42:16,922 --> 00:42:22,784
It does not reflect contraceptive failure and typically resolves within one to three cycles.

343
00:42:22,784 --> 00:42:36,147
If unscheduled bleeding persists, those with bleeding during the first part of a pill pack may benefit from an increase in the estrogen dose, whereas those with bleeding during the second part may improve with a higher progestin dose .

344
00:42:36,147 --> 00:42:39,147
# Method-specific Effects.

345
00:42:39,147 --> 00:42:46,859
Altered Drug Efficacy Some drugs decrease COC effectiveness, and choosing another contraceptive method is preferable.

346
00:42:46,859 --> 00:42:53,084
Most notable are rifampin/rifabutin, but other antibiotics are not implicated .

347
00:42:53,084 --> 00:43:06,734
Cytochrome P450–inducing anticonvulsants are another group and include phenytoin, phenobarbital, primidone, carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, topiramate, rufinamide, and felbamate .

348
00:43:06,734 --> 00:43:13,922
Some antiretroviral drugs, mainly among the protease inhibitor group, can interact with COCs.

349
00:43:13,922 --> 00:43:21,022
A rapidly evolving list is provided by the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (2019).

350
00:43:21,022 --> 00:43:24,447
In obese women, COCs are effective .

351
00:43:24,447 --> 00:43:33,609
With the Ortho Evra transdermal patch, however, obesity may alter pharmacokinetics and lower efficacy (p. 674).

352
00:43:33,609 --> 00:43:38,584
On the other hand, some COCs lower the actions of certain drugs.

353
00:43:38,584 --> 00:43:41,872
Notably, lamotrigine efficacy is

354
00:43:42,028 --> 00:43:44,403
lowered by COCs .

355
00:43:44,353 --> 00:43:53,990
Metabolic Changes, Risks, and Benefits The hormones in COCs can induce metabolic changes that may aggravate underlying medical conditions.

356
00:43:53,990 --> 00:43:59,565
Most of the contraindications to COCs reflect these metabolic alterations .

357
00:43:59,565 --> 00:44:02,228
Table 38-6.

358
00:44:02,228 --> 00:44:11,040
Contraindications to the Use of Combination Oral Contraceptive Pills Hepatic angiotensinogen synthesis is augmented by COCs.

359
00:44:11,040 --> 00:44:17,290
Its conversion by renin to angiotensin I may be associated with pill-induced hypertension.

360
00:44:17,290 --> 00:44:25,428
Thus, patients return 8 to 12 weeks after COC initiation for evaluation of blood pressure and other symptoms.

361
00:44:25,428 --> 00:44:32,540
However, women using low-dose COC formulations rarely develop clinically significant hypertension .

362
00:44:32,540 --> 00:44:48,403
In women with well-controlled hypertension, COC use is linked to greater risks than in nonusers for stroke, acute myocardial infarction, and peripheral arterial disease, and in these women, COCs are considered US MEC category 3 .

363
00:44:48,403 --> 00:44:55,215
Severe forms of hypertension, especially those with end-organ involvement, preclude COC use.

364
00:44:55,215 --> 00:45:02,190
The estrogen component of COCs boosts hepatic production of fibrinogen and many of the clotting factors.

365
00:45:02,190 --> 00:45:08,378
Deep-vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism rates are increased in women who use COCs .

366
00:45:08,378 --> 00:45:19,015
These events are estrogen-dose related, and rates have substantively declined with lower-dose formulations containing 10 to 35 μg of ethinyl estradiol.

367
00:45:19,015 --> 00:45:27,340
The general-population risk of venous thromboembolism (VTE) is 4 to 5 events per 100,000 woman-years.

368
00:45:27,340 --> 00:45:38,378
The incidence of VTE with COC use increases three- to fivefold compared with nonusers (Shaw, 2013; van Hylckama Vlieg, 2009).

369
00:45:38,378 --> 00:45:47,840
Obesity raises the general VTE risk, which is compounded by COCs (Horton, 2016; Suchon, 2016).

370
00:45:47,840 --> 00:45:53,778
Accordingly, in an obese woman, COCs are considered a US MEC category 2.

371
00:45:53,778 --> 00:46:02,315
VTE rates are also significantly increased in women smokers older than 35 years, and COCs are not recommended.

372
00:46:02,315 --> 00:46:06,965
Those most at risk for VTE include women with thrombophilias .

373
00:46:06,965 --> 00:46:15,240
Moreover, COC use during the month before a major operative procedure appears to double the risk for postoperative VTE .

374
00:46:15,240 --> 00:46:33,565
Thus, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021) recommends balancing the risks of VTE and the degree of postoperative immobility with the risk of unintended pregnancy during the 4 to 6 weeks required to reverse the thrombogenic effects of COCs before surgery.

375
00:46:33,565 --> 00:46:40,565
In the early puerperium, VTE risks are also increased, and COCs are not recommended early .

376
00:46:40,565 --> 00:46:46,003
Certain progestins within COC are also linked with greater VTE rates.

377
00:46:46,003 --> 00:46:52,478
A slightly higher VTE risk with drospirenone-containing COCs has been shown in two studies.

378
00:46:52,478 --> 00:47:05,978
In response, an assessment of benefits and VTE risks in users of these pills has been emphasized (Food and Drug Administration, 2012; Jick, 2011; Parkin, 2011).

379
00:47:05,978 --> 00:47:16,565
Desogestrel and gestodene are also implicated and carry similarly elevated risks (Stegeman, 2013; Vinogradova, 2015).

380
00:47:16,565 --> 00:47:22,165
For women with prior myocardial infarction, COCs should not be considered.

381
00:47:22,165 --> 00:47:36,428
Also, for women with multiple cardiovascular risk factors, which include smoking, hypertension, older age, dyslipidemias, and diabetes, the risk for myocardial infarction outweighs the benefits of this method.

382
00:47:36,428 --> 00:47:50,603
For women with no cardiovascular risks, low-dose oral contraceptives are not associated with an increased risk of myocardial infarction (Margolis, 2007; World Health Organization, 1997).

383
00:47:50,603 --> 00:47:55,153
For nonsmoking women younger than 35, stroke is rare .

384
00:47:55,153 --> 00:48:04,053
COCs are associated with a small increased risk for ischemic stroke (Chan, 2004; Lidegaard, 2012).

385
00:48:04,053 --> 00:48:19,153
Rates rise significantly for women who have hypertension, who smoke, or who have migraine headaches with visual aura or other focal neurological changes and use COCs (MacClellan, 2007; Tepper, 2016b).

386
00:48:19,153 --> 00:48:27,803
The evidence for stroke risk in migraineurs without aura is less clear (Etminan, 2005; Schürks, 2009).

387
00:48:27,803 --> 00:48:37,740
COC initiation may be considered for women with preexisting migraines without aura if they are otherwise healthy, younger, normotensive nonsmokers.

388
00:48:37,740 --> 00:48:43,653
For women with prior stroke, COCs should not be considered to avoid repeat events.

389
00:48:43,653 --> 00:48:49,753
Regarding carbohydrate metabolism, the risk of developing diabetes is not increased

390
00:48:49,924 --> 00:48:52,061
with COC use .

391
00:48:52,011 --> 00:49:04,186
For diabetic women, COCs may be used in nonsmokers with disease duration <20 years and without associated vascular disease, nephropathy, retinopathy, or neuropathy .

392
00:49:04,186 --> 00:49:13,499
In general, COCs raise serum triglycerides and total and HDL cholesterol levels but lower LDL cholesterol concentrations.

393
00:49:13,499 --> 00:49:18,849
Last, studies do not support a connection between COCs and weight gain .

394
00:49:18,849 --> 00:49:33,061
Regarding neoplasia, COCs offer a protective effect against ovarian and endometrial cancers (Collaborative Group on Epidemiological Studies of Ovarian Cancer, 2008; Tsilidis, 2011).

395
00:49:33,061 --> 00:49:43,411
As an exception, the relative risk of cervical dysplasia and cervical cancer is higher in current COC users, but this declines after use is discontinued.

396
00:49:43,411 --> 00:49:48,224
Following 10 or more years, risk returns to that of never users .

397
00:49:48,224 --> 00:49:53,149
It is unclear whether COCs contribute to breast cancer development.

398
00:49:53,149 --> 00:50:08,886
Major studies show no risk or a small risk among current users, which drops with time following cessation (Collaborative Group on Hormonal Factors in Breast Cancer, 1996; Hannaford, 2007; Marchbanks, 2002).

399
00:50:08,886 --> 00:50:16,186
COCs are not a risk for liver tumors (Heinemann, 1998; Maheshwari, 2007).

400
00:50:16,186 --> 00:50:27,299
For women with known tumors, COCs may be used in those with focal nodular hyperplasia, but avoided in those with benign hepatic adenoma and hepatocellular carcinoma .

401
00:50:27,299 --> 00:50:35,886
Rates of colorectal cancer appear to be reduced in ever users (Bosetti, 2009; Luan, 2015).

402
00:50:35,886 --> 00:50:44,186
Of other potential effects, cholestasis and cholestatic jaundice are uncommon and resolve when COCs are discontinued.

403
00:50:44,186 --> 00:50:55,361
In women who have active hepatitis, COCs should not be initiated, but these may be continued in women who experience a flare of their liver disease while already taking COCs.

404
00:50:55,361 --> 00:50:59,986
Use of progestin-only contraception in these women is not restricted.

405
00:50:59,986 --> 00:51:04,411
Moreover, COCs are suitable for women who have recovered.

406
00:51:04,411 --> 00:51:10,661
Mild compensated cirrhosis does not limit the use of COCs or progestin-only methods.

407
00:51:10,661 --> 00:51:15,724
With severe decompensated disease, all hormonal methods are avoided .

408
00:51:15,724 --> 00:51:24,211
Chloasma, which is hyperpigmentation of the face and forehead, is more likely in women who demonstrated such a change during pregnancy.

409
00:51:24,211 --> 00:51:28,299
This is less common with low-dose estrogen formulations.

410
00:51:28,299 --> 00:51:33,324
Previously, COCs were used to treat functional ovarian cysts.

411
00:51:33,324 --> 00:51:47,199
However, data do not support use of current low-dose COC formulations to resolve or prevent these (European Society of Human Reproduction and Embryology, 2001; Grimes, 2014).

412
00:51:47,199 --> 00:51:52,686
Last, many noncontraceptive benefits are associated with COC use .

413
00:51:52,686 --> 00:51:58,861
COCs are often selected for these effects, even in those without contraceptive needs.

414
00:51:58,861 --> 00:52:03,261
First, dysmenorrhea and heavy menstrual bleeding declines.

415
00:52:03,261 --> 00:52:08,774
COCs also elevate hepatic production of sex hormone–binding globulin.

416
00:52:08,774 --> 00:52:14,136
This binds bioavailable testosterone to diminish the action of androgens.

417
00:52:14,136 --> 00:52:18,561
Thus, conditions such as acne and hirsutism can improve.

418
00:52:18,561 --> 00:52:29,524
For women with premenstrual dysphoric disorder, drospirenone-containing COCs can lessen symptoms (Pearlstein, 2005; Yonkers, 2005).

419
00:52:29,524 --> 00:52:32,261
# Intravaginal Rings.

420
00:52:32,261 --> 00:52:40,811
NuvaRing is a flexible clear intravaginal ring that measures 54 mm in diameter and 4 mm in cross section.

421
00:52:40,811 --> 00:52:48,049
Its core releases ethinyl estradiol and etonogestrel, which are absorbed across the vaginal epithelium.

422
00:52:48,049 --> 00:52:53,836
Annovera is a new ring that releases ethinyl estradiol and segesterone acetate.

423
00:52:53,836 --> 00:53:01,149
Colored white, it measures 56 mm in diameter and 8.4 mm in cross section.

424
00:53:01,149 --> 00:53:10,724
For both, the ring is inserted within 5 days of menses onset and, after 3 weeks of use, is removed for 1 week to allow withdrawal bleeding.

425
00:53:10,724 --> 00:53:15,649
Nuvaring is single use, and after menses, a new ring is placed.

426
00:53:15,649 --> 00:53:22,749
In contrast, after removal and washing, the same Annovera ring is reinserted for another 3 weeks.

427
00:53:22,749 --> 00:53:26,486
One ring can function for 13 such cycles .

428
00:53:26,486 --> 00:53:35,199
With insertion, the ring is compressed and advanced into the vagina, but no specific final orientation within the vagina is required.

429
00:53:35,199 --> 00:53:48,886
Patient satisfaction is high with this method, although vaginitis, ring-related events, and leukorrhea are more common than with COCs (Gemzell-Danielsson, 2019; Oddsson, 2005).

430
00:53:48,886 --> 00:53:56,561
A ring may be used concurrently with vaginal medications or with a tampon (Verhoeven, 2004a,b).

431
00:53:56,561 --> 00:53:58,586
However, with

432
00:53:58,756 --> 00:54:03,318
Annovera, miconazole suppositories raised hormone levels.

433
00:54:03,268 --> 00:54:09,506
These were not altered with miconazole cream, and thus cream or oral antifungals are preferable .

434
00:54:09,506 --> 00:54:12,831
Partners may feel the ring during intercourse .

435
00:54:12,831 --> 00:54:23,106
If this is bothersome, the ring may be removed for intercourse but should be replaced within 3 hours for Nuvaring and within 2 hours for Annovera to maintain efficacy.

436
00:54:23,106 --> 00:54:25,918
# Transdermal Patches.

437
00:54:25,918 --> 00:54:32,431
The Ortho Evra patch and its generic Xulane each contain ethinyl estradiol and norelgestromin.

438
00:54:32,431 --> 00:54:37,868
A newer patch, Twirla, releases ethinyl estradiol and levonorgestrel.

439
00:54:37,868 --> 00:54:45,581
Either patch may be applied to the buttocks, upper outer arm, lower abdomen, or upper torso, but the breasts are avoided.

440
00:54:45,581 --> 00:54:53,206
Rates of application-site skin reaction are low (Kaunitz, 2015; Smallwood, 2001).

441
00:54:53,206 --> 00:54:59,343
If a patch is so poorly adhered that it requires reinforcement with tape, it should be replaced.

442
00:54:59,343 --> 00:55:08,018
Women can wear the patch in saunas and whirlpools without decreased efficacy (Abrams, 2001; Archer, 2013).

443
00:55:08,018 --> 00:55:12,331
However, with Twirla, swimming is limited to 30 minutes.

444
00:55:12,331 --> 00:55:22,293
Initiation of the patch is the same as for COCs, and a new patch is applied weekly for 3 weeks, followed by a patch-free week to allow withdrawal bleeding.

445
00:55:22,293 --> 00:55:32,518
In general, the transdermal patches and vaginal rings produce metabolic changes, side effects, and efficacy rates comparable to those with COC pills.

446
00:55:32,518 --> 00:55:45,081
However, the Ortho Evra patch has been associated with a higher VTE risk in some but not all studies (Cole, 2007; Jick, 2010; Lidegaard, 2011).

447
00:55:45,081 --> 00:55:55,268
Labeling for the patch states that the risk for VTE may be increased compared with COCs, and relative risk estimates range from 1.2 to 2.2.

448
00:55:55,268 --> 00:56:03,368
In addition, obesity—90 kg or greater—may be associated with a higher risk for patch contraceptive failure .

449
00:56:03,368 --> 00:56:11,606
In contrast, the LNG-containing patch showed comparable VTE rates compared with COCs in one randomized trial .

450
00:56:11,606 --> 00:56:16,656
Moreover, efficacy was similar between obese and normal-weight users.

451
00:56:16,656 --> 00:56:18,631
Barrier Methods.

452
00:56:18,631 --> 00:56:20,956
# Male Condom.

453
00:56:20,956 --> 00:56:30,543
For many years, male and female condoms, vaginal diaphragms, and periodic abstinence have been used for contraception with variable success.

454
00:56:30,543 --> 00:56:39,506
When used properly, condoms provide considerable but not absolute protection against a broad range of STDs, including HIV .

455
00:56:39,506 --> 00:56:46,318
Contraceptive efficacy of the male condom is enhanced by a reservoir tip and by the addition of a spermicide.

456
00:56:46,318 --> 00:56:52,706
Adjunct lubricant and spermicides should be water-based because oil-based products degrade latex.

457
00:56:52,706 --> 00:57:01,156
For individuals sensitive to latex, condoms made from lamb intestines are effective, but they do not provide infection protection.

458
00:57:01,156 --> 00:57:07,781
Instead, nonallergenic condoms made of polyurethane or of synthetic elastomers are available.

459
00:57:07,781 --> 00:57:15,656
Polyurethane condoms are effective against STDs but have a higher breakage and slippage rate compared with latex condoms .

460
00:57:15,656 --> 00:57:18,143
# Female Condom.

461
00:57:18,143 --> 00:57:24,656
The only female condom available in the United States is marketed as the FC2 Female Condom.

462
00:57:24,656 --> 00:57:30,231
It contains a nitrile sheath and outer ring and a flexible polyurethane inner ring.

463
00:57:30,231 --> 00:57:38,131
Its open ring remains outside the vagina, whereas its closed internal ring is fitted under the symphysis like a diaphragm .

464
00:57:38,131 --> 00:57:44,656
The inner and outer sheath are covered with siliconebased lubricant, and other-based lubricants can be added.

465
00:57:44,656 --> 00:57:53,781
Male condoms should not be used concurrently because simultaneous use creates friction that leads to condom slipping, tearing, and displacement.

466
00:57:53,781 --> 00:58:00,581
Following use, the female condom outer ring should be twisted to seal the condom so that no semen spills.

467
00:58:00,581 --> 00:58:06,368
As an added value, the female condom may offer some protection against STDs .

468
00:58:06,368 --> 00:58:08,893
Figure 38-3.

469
00:58:08,893 --> 00:58:12,731
FC2 Female Condom insertion and positioning.

470
00:58:12,731 --> 00:58:16,331
A. The inner ring is squeezed for insertion.

471
00:58:16,331 --> 00:58:19,856
The sheath is inserted similarly to a diaphragm.

472
00:58:19,856 --> 00:58:24,068
B. The inner ring is pushed inward with an index finger.

473
00:58:24,068 --> 00:58:27,218
# Diaphragm plus Spermicide.

474
00:58:27,218 --> 00:58:34,681
The diaphragm consists of a latex dome of various diameters supported by a circular latex-covered metal spring.

475
00:58:34,681 --> 00:58:43,443
It is effective when used in combination with water-based spermicidal jelly or cream, which is applied into the dome cup and along the device rim.

476
00:58:43,443 --> 00:58:48,218
The diaphragm is then positioned so that the cup faces the cervix and the

477
00:58:48,388 --> 00:58:53,513
cervix is partitioned effectively from the remainder of the vagina and the penis.

478
00:58:53,463 --> 00:58:58,688
In this fashion, the centrally placed spermicide is held against the cervix.

479
00:58:58,688 --> 00:59:10,213
When appropriately positioned, one rim is lodged deep in the posterior vaginal fornix, and the opposite rim fits behind the inner surface of the symphysis and immediately below the urethra.

480
00:59:10,213 --> 00:59:14,325
If a diaphragm is too small, it will not remain in place.

481
00:59:14,325 --> 00:59:18,813
If it is too large, it is uncomfortable when forced into position.

482
00:59:18,813 --> 00:59:24,375
Coexistent pelvic organ prolapse typically leads to instability and expulsion.

483
00:59:24,375 --> 00:59:32,888
Because size and spring flexibility must be individualized, the diaphragm is fitted by providers and available only by prescription.

484
00:59:32,888 --> 00:59:37,663
The diaphragm and spermicide can be inserted hours before intercourse.

485
00:59:37,663 --> 00:59:45,838
If more than 6 hours elapse, the diaphragm can remain but additional spermicide is placed in the vagina for maximum protection.

486
00:59:45,838 --> 00:59:49,888
Spermicide is reapplied before each coital episode.

487
00:59:49,888 --> 00:59:54,713
The diaphragm is not removed for at least 6 hours after intercourse.

488
00:59:54,713 --> 01:00:06,075
Because toxic shock syndrome has been described following its use, it may be worthwhile to remove the diaphragm at 6 hours, or at least the next morning, to minimize this rare event.

489
01:00:06,075 --> 01:00:15,513
Diaphragm use is associated with a slightly greater rate of urinary infections, presumably from urethral irritation by the ring under the symphysis.

490
01:00:15,513 --> 01:00:22,000
# Cervical Cap. FemCap is the only cervical cap currently available in the United States.

491
01:00:22,000 --> 01:00:32,938
Made of silicone rubber, it has a sailor-cap shape with a dome that covers the cervix and a flared brim, which allows the cap to be held in place by the upper vagina’s muscular walls.

492
01:00:32,938 --> 01:00:43,788
Available in 22-, 26-, and 30-mm sizes, it is used with a spermicide applied once at insertion to both sides of the dome cup.

493
01:00:43,788 --> 01:00:51,388
For contraception, it should remain in place for 6 hours following coitus and may remain for up to 48 hours.

494
01:00:51,388 --> 01:00:58,300
Even with proper fitting and correct use, pregnancy rates with this method are higher than those with the diaphragm .

495
01:00:58,300 --> 01:01:01,313
Fertility Awareness–Based Methods.

496
01:01:01,313 --> 01:01:07,813
These attempt to identify the fertile days each cycle and advise sexual abstinence during these days.

497
01:01:07,813 --> 01:01:15,275
Fertility awareness–based methods (FABMs) as a group have a 15-percent pregnancy rate with typical use .

498
01:01:15,275 --> 01:01:24,425
Some smartphone applications aim to assist these practices (Berglund Scherwitzl, 2017; Jennings, 2019).

499
01:01:24,425 --> 01:01:31,450
The Standard Days Method counsels women to avoid unprotected intercourse during cycle days 8 through 19.

500
01:01:31,450 --> 01:01:37,850
For successful use, women must have regular monthly cycles of 26 to 32 days.

501
01:01:37,850 --> 01:01:45,925
Those who use this method can mark a calendar or can use Cycle-Beads, which is a ring of counting beads, to keep track of their days.

502
01:01:45,925 --> 01:01:55,800
The TwoDay Method relies on awareness of vaginal wetness, which reflects changes in the amount and quality of cervical mucus at different times in the menstrual cycle.

503
01:01:55,800 --> 01:02:02,475
Intercourse is considered safe if a woman did not note mucus on the day of planned intercourse or the day prior.

504
01:02:02,475 --> 01:02:14,600
The Symptothermal Method combines changes in cervical mucus—onset of the fertile period; changes in basal body temperature—end of the fertile period; and calculations to estimate ovulation.

505
01:02:14,600 --> 01:02:21,675
A sustained 0.4°F rise in the basal body temperature usually precedes ovulation.

506
01:02:21,675 --> 01:02:30,100
For maximum efficacy, the woman must abstain from intercourse from the first day of menses through the third day after the temperature increase.

507
01:02:30,100 --> 01:02:37,100
Overall, this method is more complex to learn and apply, and it does not appreciably improve efficacy.

508
01:02:37,100 --> 01:02:39,013
Spermicides.

509
01:02:39,013 --> 01:02:48,025
Most spermicides contain nonoxynol-9 and are sold over-the-counter as creams, jellies, suppositories, films, and foams.

510
01:02:48,025 --> 01:02:55,288
They are a less effective method but provide a chemical spermicidal action and a physical barrier to sperm penetration.

511
01:02:55,288 --> 01:03:02,838
Their duration of maximal effectiveness is usually no more than 1 hour, and these do not offer STD protection.

512
01:03:02,838 --> 01:03:07,713
If pregnancy does occur, nonoxynol-9 is not teratogenic .

513
01:03:07,713 --> 01:03:17,588
In 2020, a vaginal contraceptive gel containing lactic acid, citric acid, and potassium bitartrate was FDA approved as Phexxi.

514
01:03:17,588 --> 01:03:24,688
As an acidifying agent, it resists the buffering effect of semen and thereby allows the vagina’s normally

515
01:03:24,844 --> 01:03:31,644
acidic pH to work as a spermicide (Garg, 2001; Nelson, 2018).

516
01:03:31,594 --> 01:03:37,706
This pH effect also has potential as a microbicide, which warrants additional study.

517
01:03:37,706 --> 01:03:43,594
Ideally, either agent is deposited high in the vagina up to 1 hour before coitus.

518
01:03:43,594 --> 01:03:48,344
Thereafter, each must be reinserted before repeat intercourse.

519
01:03:48,344 --> 01:03:51,194
# Contraceptive Sponge.

520
01:03:51,194 --> 01:03:56,731
The Today contraceptive sponge is an over-the-counter, one-size-fits-all device.

521
01:03:56,731 --> 01:04:04,994
The nonoxynol-9–impregnated polyurethane disc is 2.5 cm thick and 5.5 cm wide.

522
01:04:04,994 --> 01:04:10,956
One side has a dimple that faces the cervix, and the other has a satin loop for removal.

523
01:04:10,956 --> 01:04:20,469
The sponge can be inserted up to 24 hours prior to intercourse, and while in place, it provides contraception regardless of coital frequency.

524
01:04:20,469 --> 01:04:24,506
It should remain in place for 6 hours after intercourse.

525
01:04:24,506 --> 01:04:32,044
Pregnancy is prevented primarily by the spermicide and to a lesser extent by covering the cervix and absorbing semen.

526
01:04:32,044 --> 01:04:38,581
Although the sponge is possibly more convenient than the diaphragm or condom, it is less effective than either .

527
01:04:38,581 --> 01:04:44,906
Most common causes for method discontinuance are pregnancy, discomfort, or vaginitis .

528
01:04:44,906 --> 01:04:57,094
Although toxic shock syndrome has been reported with the contraceptive sponge, it is rare, and evidence suggests that the sponge may actually limit production of the responsible staphylococcal exotoxin .

529
01:04:57,094 --> 01:05:02,931
Still, it is recommended that the sponge not be used during menses or the puerperium.

530
01:05:02,931 --> 01:05:05,481
Emergency Contraception.

531
01:05:05,481 --> 01:05:15,556
Following unprotected sexual intercourse, several emergency contraception (EC) regimens substantially lower the likelihood of an unwanted pregnancy.

532
01:05:15,556 --> 01:05:24,419
Current methods include COCs, a progestin, a selective progesteronereceptor modulator (SPRM), and the Cu-IUD .

533
01:05:24,419 --> 01:05:32,669
Notably, the single-dose LNG pill is available over-the-counter without a prescription to all reproductive-aged individuals.

534
01:05:32,669 --> 01:05:45,881
Patients can obtain information regarding EC by calling 888-NOT-2-LATE or accessing The Emergency Contraception Website: http://not-2-late.com.

535
01:05:45,881 --> 01:05:54,531
Although not yet adopted as EC, early efficacy data find the LNG-IUS not inferior to the Cu-IUD for EC .

536
01:05:54,531 --> 01:05:57,144
Table 38-7.

537
01:05:57,144 --> 01:06:04,044
Emergency Contraception Methods and Some Brand-Name Examples # Hormonal Emergency Contraception.

538
01:06:04,044 --> 01:06:11,656
Except for allergy to a particular component, no conditions in the US MEC contraindicate hormonal EC methods.

539
01:06:11,656 --> 01:06:18,269
Moreover, clinical examination or pregnancy testing is not required before EC provision .

540
01:06:18,269 --> 01:06:25,881
With all methods, dosing begins as early as possible and ideally within 72 hours of unprotected coitus.

541
01:06:25,881 --> 01:06:35,606
It may be given up to 120 hours after coitus (Fine, 2010; Rodrigues, 2001; von Hertzen, 2002).

542
01:06:35,606 --> 01:06:41,319
The major mechanism with all hormonal regimens is inhibition or delay of ovulation.

543
01:06:41,319 --> 01:06:50,344
Of oral methods, failure rates are lowest with ulipristal (1 to 2 percent) and greatest with the Yuzpe method (2 to 3.5 percent) .

544
01:06:50,344 --> 01:07:05,206
If EC fails to prevent pregnancy or is mistimed, no associations with major congenital malformation or pregnancy complications have been noted with these hormonal methods (Jatlaoui, 2016; Levy, 2014).

545
01:07:05,206 --> 01:07:10,544
With EC administration, nausea and vomiting can be an important side effect.

546
01:07:10,544 --> 01:07:16,619
Accordingly, an oral antiemetic may be prescribed at least 1 hour before each dose .

547
01:07:16,619 --> 01:07:21,406
If a woman vomits within 2 hours of a dose, the dose is repeated.

548
01:07:21,406 --> 01:07:27,769
For subsequent coitus, a barrier method is recommended until long-term contraception is begun.

549
01:07:27,769 --> 01:07:32,444
If not, EC can be repeated within a given menstrual cycle.

550
01:07:32,444 --> 01:07:39,169
Quick start initiation of contraceptive methods is suitable and is discussed in the respective method sections.

551
01:07:39,169 --> 01:07:48,794
Notably, because ulipristal competes at the progesterone receptor, a 5-day delay is recommended before starting progestin-containing contraceptives.

552
01:07:48,794 --> 01:07:52,581
# Copper-containing Intrauterine Devices.

553
01:07:52,581 --> 01:08:01,631
For women who are candidates, Cu-IUD insertion is the most effective EC method and provides an effective 10-year form of contraception.

554
01:08:01,631 --> 01:08:13,619
If an IUD is placed up to 5 days after unprotected coitus, the pregnancy rate approximates only 0.1 percent (Cleland, 2012; Wu, 2010).

